          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %strong tangent bundle category
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            equipped with a “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+bundle"}> tangent bundle
            ”
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/endofunctor"} endofunctor
            satisfying some natural axioms.
          %p
            Usually these are called simply
            = succeed "," do
              %strong tangent categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+category"} tangent category
            is about “the tangent category of a given category” constructed by abelianization. In other words, tangent bundle categories are about
            %em abstraction
            of the tangent bundle construction, while tangent categories are a
            %em categorification
            thereof (in some vague sense).
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            Tangent bundle categories have three equivalent definitions: the first is due to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ji%C5%99%C3%AD+Rosick%C3%BD"} Jiří Rosický
            and was rediscovered/refined by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robin+Cockett"} Cockett
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Geoff+Cruttwell"} Cruttwell
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+Garner"} Richard Garner
            gave a definition of a tangent category as a sort of enriched category.
          %h3#classical_definition Classical Definition
          %p
            Tangent bundle categories were originally introduced by Rosicky to model the behaviour of the tangent bundle on the category of smooth manifolds, and of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/microlinear+spaces"} microlinear spaces
            in a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+topos"} smooth topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            and a point
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi M
