          %p These morphisms are important in the axiomatization of differential structure given in cartesian differential categories.
          %h4#additive_bundles Additive bundles
          %p Tangent categories were originally defined by Rosicky using abelian group bundles, however Cockett and Cruttwell’s definition uses commutative monoid bundles in order to capture examples of tangent structure that arise in theoretical computer science.
          %p
            An additive bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                  %mo :
                  %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q: E \to B
            in a category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝕏
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{X}
            is a commutative monoid in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕏
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{X}/B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi q
                  %mo →
                  %mi q
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f,g): q \to q'
            is
            %em additive
            if it preserves the fibered commutative monoid structure.
