          %h3#tangent_structure_as_abstract_weil_prolongation Tangent Structure as abstract Weil Prolongation
          %p There is a vast literature on the notion of a “Weil functor”. A particularly important theorem is that every product preserving endofunctor on the category of smooth manifolds is given by a prolongation operation with a Weil algebra. To simplify this section, we will only consider the case of tangent categories with negatives - see Leung’s thesis to see the generalization to commutative rigs.
          %h4#weil_algebras Weil Algebras
          %p
            Consider a commutative ring
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            = succeed "-algebra." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %em Weil Algebra
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            is an augmented
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            so that:
          %ol
            %li
              The underlying
              = succeed "-module" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              is
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi R
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R^n
            %li
              The
              %em kernel of augmentation
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                    %mo :
                    %mi V
                    %mo →
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi: V \to R
              is nilpotent: there exists a natural number
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              so that for every
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                        %mi ker
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi π
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in \mathsf{ker}(\pi)
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mi k
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x^k = 0
          %p
            The category of Weil algebras is the full subcategory of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo −
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                    %mi alg
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R-\mathsf{alg}/R
            whose objects are Weil algebras.
          #cat_of_R_weil.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %ol
              %li
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                \-Weil algebras have products.
              %li
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                \-Weil algebras have coproducts.
              %li
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                is a zero object in the category of
                = succeed "-Weil" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
          %p
            It is often useful to consider a presentation of
            = succeed "-Weil" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
          #presentation_of_R_weil.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  (1) Weil algebras may be presented as
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi R
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %msub
                            %mi X
                            %mi i
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mi I
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R[X_i]/I
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi I
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
                  is an ideal of
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi R
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %msub
                            %mi X
                            %mi i
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R[X_i]
              %li
                %p
                  (2) The product of Weil algebras
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mi i
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi I
                        %mo ,
                        %mi R
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %msub
                          %mi Y
                          %mi j
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi J
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R[X_i]/I, R[Y_j]/J
                  may be presented as
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi R
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %msub
                            %mi X
                            %mi i
                          %mo ,
                          %msub
                            %mi Y
                            %mi j
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi I
                          %mo ∪
                          %mi J
                          %mo ∪
                          %mi XY
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R[X_i,Y_j]/(I \cup J \cup XY)
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi XY
